{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788367431,"nonce":"g77AlnqiBbo3VKR8AlCsHVWGeBY=","title":"Run `m1-20260901`: `build` failed with `bad-args` **245 times**. Reading the details rather than the count:","message":"```\n238  'DeepDrill' is not made from stuff\n  3  'Grave' is not made from stuff\n  4  advance '\u003cid\u003e' in flight\n```\n\n**238 of them are the same call, refused the same way, repeated.** The agent passed `stuff` to a `DeepDrill`, was told no, and made the identical call 238 more times across the run. Nothing stopped it, nothing escalated, and nothing in any envelope said \"you have now been told this 200 times.\"\n\n\nThe refusal was correct every time. `DeepDrill` genuinely is not made from stuff, and the message says so plainly. The defect is that **an unattended agent can loop on a deterministic refusal indefinitely** and the protocol has no opinion about it.\n\n`5cb1f9f` already argues the same shape from the other end: *\"N consecutive `dialog` halts on the same window type is a wedge, not a series of events, and should be reported as one.\"* That issue's `PLAY-LOOP.md` note adds the reason the existing guard misses it — the two-consecutive-0-tick rule does not fire, because each of these calls does something.\n\nThe two cases together say the rule is general: **a repeated identical outcome is one event, and the protocol should say so.**\n\n\n1. The mod tracks, per `(op, error code, normalised args)`, how many times in a row that exact refusal has been returned, and publishes the count on the envelope once it exceeds a small threshold. Not a refusal — a field the caller cannot miss, in the `ignored_args` idiom.\n2. The threshold is justified in the code comment rather than guessed at, and it is stated in ticks-or-calls terms that match how a driver loops.\n3. It resets when the call succeeds, when the arguments change, or when the game state the refusal depends on changes.\n4. `checklists/turn.md` gains the trip-wire: the same refusal N times is a wedge, escalate rather than retry.\n5. `accept/\u003cid\u003e.py` or a bench command list: issue the same refused call N+1 times, assert the field appears and names the count; change one argument, assert it resets.\n\n\nDeliberately narrower than a general rate limiter. The mod should not refuse to answer, and should not decide how long a driver may loop; it should only make the fact visible, because today the only place the repetition exists is in a transcript nobody reads until the run is over.","files":null}]}